independent_verification.evidence:
Tested on an actual file rather than the regex alone. Wrote a multi-line literal into promotions.ts with the field on its own line and the closing brace two lines below — 'return {
  items: r.items,
  next_cursor: r.next_cursor,
  has_more
}' — and source-scan FAILED, so detection works exactly as section 7.1 claims. The mechanism is the one the design names: findViolationInFile tests whole-file content first and only then walks lines to attach a number. The caveat is what happens at that second step — no single line matches, so the function falls through to its 'Multi-line pattern: no single line matches — report line 1' branch, and the violation was reported as 'web/src/api/promotions.ts:1'. Detection is correct and the build goes red with the pattern named, so nothing is missed; but a reader chasing line 1 of a 300-line file will be briefly confused. This is pre-existing source-scan behaviour shared with the missing-query-state-boundary pattern, not something this design introduced. Worth one sentence in section 7.1 so the next reader is not puzzled by a line-1 report.
